### 🛠️ Key Recommended Fixes
#### 1. `src/Cache.cpp:15` (CRITICAL)
When inserting a new key into a full cache, `last = items_list_.back()` is computed and popped from `items_list_`, but `items_map_[last.first]` is never erased. This leaves a stale entry in `items_map_` pointing to a destroyed iterator. Subsequent lookups will use a dangling iterator, and the map grows unboundedly across evictions.

```cpp
Capture the key before popping, then erase it from the map:

```if (items_map_.size() >= capacity_) {
  auto last = items_list_.back();
  items_map_.erase(last.first);
  items_list_.pop_back();
}

```

#### 2. `src/Cache.cpp:15` (CRITICAL)
Additionally, the branch order is wrong for the update path: when a key already exists, the code erases from `items_list_` but never removes from `items_map_` before re-inserting. The re-insert `items_map_[std::string(key)] = items_list_.begin();` works, but the value-insert path above must also erase-from-map, otherwise the map accumulates dead entries on every fresh insertion at capacity.

```cpp
Restructure to always erase the old map entry before inserting:

```auto it = items_map_.find(std::string(key));
if (it != items_map_.end()) {
  items_map_.erase(it);
  items_list_.erase(it->second);
} else if (items_map_.size() >= capacity_) {
  auto last = items_list_.back();
  items_map_.erase(last.first);
  items_list_.pop_back();
}
items_list_.push_front({std::string(key), std::string(value)});
items_map_.emplace(key, items_list_.begin());

```

#### 3. `src/Cache.cpp:8` (WARNING)
`std::string(key)` is constructed up to 4 times per `set()` (in `find`, push_front key, emplace key). Each copy allocates. Use `items_map_.try_emplace` or store the constructed key once to reduce allocations on the hot write path — relevant since this PR explicitly targets throughput.

```cpp
Construct the key once into a local `std::string` and reuse it for lookups and insertions.
```

#### 4. `src/Cache.cpp:25` (CRITICAL)
`get()` uses `std::shared_lock` for the entire call, including the `list_.splice(...)` which mutates the linked list under a shared lock. `std::list::splice` is documented to invalidate iterators of the spliced-from list but does not synchronize against writers; with concurrent `set()` calls mutating the list, this is a data race. Readers can observe a partially-updated list.

```cpp
Either use `std::unique_lock` for `get()`, or split the lookup (shared_lock) from the LRU promotion (unique_lock) — promote only if needed and consider skipping promotion under a shared lock to keep the path lock-free for reads.
```

